<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Chancellor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Chancellor was an English navigator who in 1553 sailed with Sir Hugh Willoughby's expedition in search of a north-east passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Separated from the other ships, he reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> and travelled overland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> received him and granted trading privileges, leading to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a>. He drowned in 1556 when his ship was wrecked off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on the return from a second voyage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-chancellor.jpg" alt="Richard Chancellor" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.22,_p._243_-_English_ambassadors.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chancellor, a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, was brought up in the household of Sir William Sidney. In 1550 he sailed as an apprentice pilot to the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in the bark Aucher under Roger Bodenham, and he learned more geography and seamanship from the explorer Sebastian Cabot and the geographer John Dee. Cabot had long wanted to reach Asia through the Arctic, and in 1552-1553 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> merchants chartered the Company of Merchant Adventurers for the purpose, with the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> as principal patron, hoping also to find a market for English woollen cloth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Chancellor went as chief navigator and second-in-command to Sir Hugh Willoughby, under orders from Cabot to behave peaceably and keep a journal. A violent storm off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-cape">North Cape</a> separated the ships. Willoughby sailed east, reached Novaya Zemlya and died with all his men during the winter on the Kola Peninsula. Chancellor, in the Edward Bonaventure, called at Vardø, where Scottish fishermen warned him of the dangers ahead, then found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> and anchored at the mouth of the Dvina River, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> now stands.</p><p>Invited by Tsar Ivan the Terrible, he travelled over 600 miles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> by sleigh. He found the city much larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and built mostly of wood, the tsar's palace and dinners luxurious. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> then had no access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> held a monopoly of its trade with the West, so the tsar welcomed a sea route and gave Chancellor letters inviting English traders and promising them privileges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chancellor returned in the summer of 1554 to find King Edward dead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> executed by Mary. No stigma attached to him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a>, as the association was now called, sent him back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> in 1555. He learned what had become of Willoughby, recovered his papers and spent the season organising trade and asking how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> might be reached by the northern route.</p><p>In July 1556 he sailed for home with Osip Nepeya, the first Russian ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in a fleet of four ships. Off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> the weather turned: the Bona Esperanza sank, the Bona Confidentia was never heard of again, and only the Philip and Mary reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in April 1557. The Edward Bonaventure was driven ashore at Pitsligo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> on 10 November 1556, and Chancellor died with most of the crew. Nepeya and a few others survived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Chancellor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade grew steadily: the English navigator <b>Richard Chancellor</b> reached the White Sea in 1553, the Muscovy Company was chartered in London in 1555, and Arkhangelsk, founded in 1584, became the main port for trade with western Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.45,-2.6&amp;year=1546" aria-label="Bristol on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Richard Chancellor"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4862 5725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4862 5725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.45,-2.6&amp;year=1546">What was here in 1546? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
